<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walvis Bay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walvis Bay is a port town on the Atlantic coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a>, the only good natural harbour on that desert shore. In the 19th century it was the outlet for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and gun trade of the interior, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> annexed the bay in 1878, later attaching it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>. It remained a South African enclave after Namibian independence in 1990 and was transferred to Namibia in 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walvis-bay.jpg" alt="Walvis Bay" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Brian McMorrow · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Walvis_Bay_aerial.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Walvis Bay lies just north of the Tropic of Capricorn in the delta of the Kuiseb River. The bay is protected by the Pelican Point sand spit and is the only natural deep-water harbour on the country's coast. Its waters are rich in plankton and marine life and draw large numbers of southern right whales, which attracted whalers and fishing vessels. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> called it Walvisch Baye and the English Whale Bay, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herero-people">Herero people</a> called it Ezorongondo. The climate is a rare mild form of cold desert, with about 13 millimetres of rain a year and high humidity.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Portuguese navigator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diogo-cao">Diogo Cão</a> reached Cape Cross, north of the bay, in 1485, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a> anchored his flagship São Cristóvão in the bay on 8 December 1487, naming it O Golfo de Santa Maria da Conceição. The Portuguese made no formal claim. At the end of the 18th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> used it as a stopover between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, but no year-round settlement was attempted and little commercial development took place until the late 19th century.</p><p>During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scramble-for-africa">Scramble for Africa</a> the British claimed the bay, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> completed its annexation in 1884, together with the Penguin Islands, after initial steps in 1878, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> annexed the surrounding land as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a>. In 1910 the bay became part of the Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> as an exclave of the Cape. A boundary dispute with Germany was settled in 1911, with a confirmed area of 1,124 square kilometres. The Germans overran it early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, and South African forces ousted them in 1915.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">South West Africa</a> under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a>, and in 1922 an Act placed Walvis Bay's administration under the mandated territory. In 1977 South Africa repealed it and returned control to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-province">Cape Province</a>, making the bay an exclave. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a> in Resolution 432 of 1978 called for its reintegration into Namibia. It stayed under South African sovereignty after Namibian independence in 1990. In 1992 the two countries set up a Joint Administrative Authority, and South Africa transferred sovereignty of Walvis Bay and the Penguin Islands to Namibia on 1 March 1994.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Walvis Bay is the second largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">Namibia</a> and its largest coastal city, with 102,704 people at the 2023 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It lies at the end of the TransNamib Railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windhoek">Windhoek</a>. Under South African administration it was developed as a segregated town, with Black residents in Kuisebmond, Coloured residents in Narraville and Whites in the centre. Fishing and the port form a major part of its economy, and tourists come for Dune 7, the salt works, birdlife and Bird Island.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Walvis Bay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jonker's mounted gunmen raided Herero cattle and controlled trade routes to <b>Walvis Bay</b>, where cattle were exchanged for guns, horses and brandy.</blockquote><a href="/state/orlam-afrikaner-state">Orlam Afrikaner State</a></li></ul></section>
